// %%
md`## Design

The flowchart below is drawn by mermaid, which loads from a CDN: offline it shows \`unable to load module\`.`
// %%
architecture = onPaper(mermaid`flowchart LR
  snap[(snapshot tables + keys)] --> keys[compile: boxes, lines, hulls]
  keys --> lv[levels per axis]
  ord[(overlay: order fkey)] --> lv
  lv --> rk[ranks: column order or SCC + longest path]
  rk --> paths[x path, y path per row]
  folds[folds: y prefixes] --> q[quotient: rows under a folded prefix become one group]
  paths --> q
  q --> lift[lift edges: group by visible ends, sum base rows]
  q --> geo[geometry: column widths from cell occupancy]
  lift --> svg[SVG string]
  geo --> svg
  svg -. click label .-> folds
  svg -. drag in cell .-> ord`)
// %%
md`### How a row gets its place

1. Every level gives the row a value: the column's value, or its rank along the relation. A relation is condensed into strongly connected components first, so a cycle gets one rank; rank is the longest path from a source, ties visited in id order.
2. The y values form the row's y path, the x values its x path. Distinct y paths are rows of the grid and distinct x paths are columns, both sorted lexicographically (numbers before strings, strings by code unit, never \`localeCompare\`).
3. Rows sharing both paths share a cell and are ordered inside it by the view's \`cellSort\` columns, then human key, then id.
4. A folded y prefix replaces every row under it with one group. The group's row is the prefix itself and it spans from its leftmost to its rightmost member's column. With \`view.cards\`, a group whose members share one column is instead a box in its parent prefix's row, ordered with that row's boxes. A fold is a prefix, a level number (every prefix of that length), or \`{open: prefix}\` (an exception to a level fold).
5. Column width is the largest cell occupancy in that column across the visible rows, so folding reflows widths as well as rows.`
// %%
md`### Decisions

| decided | instead of | because |
|---|---|---|
| input is a snapshot of tables | live entities from a Cloud Brain endpoint (interview answer, 2026-10-25) | Tom, 2026-10-26: "really it should work off a snapshot"; the RPC map is test data |
| schema-agnostic: a named map of arrays of fixed-shape rows | Cloud Brain lexicons as the vocabulary (2026-10-25) | "its a general diagram drawing tool, so the source is probably something morphic to a relational model" |
| one model: every diagram is boxes and lines read from tables by their keys | nodes and edges built in JS per diagram (\`brainRowsOf\`, \`withMethods\`, \`tinyRowsOf\`) | Tom, 2026-11-03: "yes move everything to tables so we have one conceptual model in play" |
| boxes and lines come from the tables' keys, no query language | SQL queries (sql.js), chosen 2026-10-26 ("lets do real SQL I think it will make it more readable") | Tom, 2026-11-03: "yes lets eliminate SQL now, it simplifies things". Every query had a JS twin the diagrams drew from, sql.js needed the network, and depth along a relation stayed in JS |
| axes are ordinal levels, no numeric spacing | numeric scales | "the axis are not spaced numerically, so they are collapsable" |
| every level is an order relation; a column is the total-order case | attribute axes and graph axes as separate kinds | Tom: "Maybe its more consistent if edges ARE the coordinate system?" |
| a drag writes a fractional key to an overlay table | a pairwise "C below B" row per drag (Tom's first pick) | Tom: "fraction key is simpler"; it cannot form a cycle. Also "human edits cannot change the truth of the dataset" |
| id is always the last tie break | allowing boxes to stack | "we always need tie breakers to avoid stacking on top, even if its some default system id" |
| deterministic layout, no solver | constraint or force solver | Tom, 2026-10-26: "it needs to be a deterministic layout" |
| fold = truncate a y prefix; lift = group by visible ends, count | Datalog lifting rules (interview answer, 2026-10-25) | a lift is a group-by on the visible ends; renaming a relation as it lifts is not built |
| a line between a box and a box nested in it is kept | Holt's lift, which removes ancestor and descendant pairs (\`HLU := (D o U o A) - ID - D - A\`, Fahmy, Holt, Cordy 2001) | a Worker calling a method drawn inside another Worker is the call graph of chapter 3; the two definitions agree wherever no row is drawn inside another drawn row: R14's test draws parent boxes only, and on the 2026-11-03 fixture no chapter draws a line between a row and its ancestor (cb4 has no Worker calling its own method) |
| docs against data is a compare of two relations | data and docs lines drawn side by side, never compared (before 2026-11-03) | Tom, 2026-11-03: "Maybe they do some things better we can integrate"; reflexion models are the closest published form |
| a level named \`id\`, \`label\`, \`kind\` or \`href\` is refused | storing it and overwriting the box's field | found 2026-11-03: \`{ col: "id" }\` replaced every box's id, and every line was hidden |
| folds and order are keyed by view name | one shared fold state | Tom, 2026-10-27: "hard to see its value without multiple views… interesting to see the code/infra grouped by compute type vs. call graph" |
| folded group is a span bar over its members' range | a point at an aggregate value | Tom's answer 2026-10-25 |
| fold groups are the levels the drawer chose | a fixed containment tree | "possibly an aesthetic choice up to the diagram drawer" |
| rows missing an axis value are excluded and counted | a parking strip, or inferring a value | Tom's answer 2026-10-25 |
| n-ary relations draw as hulls | hub nodes or metro lines | Tom's answer 2026-10-25 |
| expand reflows in place | drill down, fisheye | Tom's answer 2026-10-25 |

### Known traps

- \`localeCompare\` depends on the browser's locale, so it would break R1 across machines. The engine compares by code unit.
- A convex hull of a flow's boxes covers boxes that are not in the flow. Seen 2026-10-26 in the prototype: the 'inbox.poll path' hull covers kernel.session. A hull that hugs only its members is more work and is not built.
- A curve between two boxes in one row passes behind any box between them and reads as an edge from that box. The 2026-10-27 review found three: tab.ui → kernel.xrpc looked like kernel.session → kernel.xrpc, and likewise core.route → core.decide and the calls into rows.get. The engine now arcs a same-row edge over the row, and attaches a cross-row edge's end at the top or bottom of its box when a box sits between the ends in that row. Two opposite edges between the same boxes (core.route ⇄ the folded services bar) drew on one line; they are now offset 5 px each way, with the count beside the line.
- A cycle outline drawn around a folded group covers the whole bar. The engine draws the outline only while every member is its own box; folded, the cycle shows as lifted edges in both directions.
- \`Map\` and \`Set\` iterate in insertion order, which is snapshot row order. Every iteration the engine's output depends on is sorted first; R1's test reverses the rows to catch a miss.`
// %%
md`### Prior art

Read 2026-11-03 by three research agents, each citation checked against its DOI or Crossref record and, where marked, the paper's own text. The 2026-10-26 list it replaces was from a web search and memory.

**What each part of the model is called**

| here | published as | source | read |
|---|---|---|---|
| y and x level lists | *nest*: "only creates set entries for which there exist records with those domain values"; the grid is the *cross* of the two axes | Stolte, Tang, Hanrahan, [Polaris](https://graphics.stanford.edu/papers/polaris_extended/polaris.pdf), IEEE TVCG 8(1) 2002, §4.1 | full text |
| fold | *roll-up*: nodes equal on every dimension in D become one node; edge weight is the sum over the members | Wattenberg, [PivotGraph](https://doi.org/10.1145/1124772.1124891), CHI 2006 | full text |
| \`where\` | *selection*: the induced subgraph on chosen attribute values | same | full text |
| lifting through \`nest\` | *lift*: \`(D o U o A) - ID - D - A\`, with \`D = C+\`, \`A = inv(C)+\` | Fahmy, Holt, Cordy, [Wins and Losses of Algebraic Transformations of Software Architectures](https://doi.org/10.1109/ASE.2001.989790), ASE 2001; Holt, [Structural manipulations of software architecture using Tarski relational algebra](https://doi.org/10.1109/WCRE.1998.723191), WCRE 1998 | ASE 2001 full text; WCRE 1998 citation only |
| \`compare\` | *reflexion model*: convergences, divergences, absences of a high-level model against a mapped source model | Murphy, Notkin, Sullivan, [Software reflexion models](https://doi.org/10.1145/222124.222136), FSE 1995 | full text |
| depth along a relation | longest-path layering after merging each cycle; the Graphviz paper rejects merging cycles ("more informative drawings than would be obtained by collapsing all the nodes in a cycle") and minimises edge length instead | Gansner, Koutsofios, North, Vo, [A technique for drawing directed graphs](https://doi.org/10.1109/32.221135), IEEE TSE 1993 | full text |
| deterministic layout | "Hive plots are predictable: for a given set of rules, the same structures always have the same layout" | Krzywinski et al., [Hive plots](https://doi.org/10.1093/bib/bbr069), Briefings in Bioinformatics 2012 | full text |
| placement by attribute | "non-overlapping regions in which node placement is based on node attributes" | Shneiderman, Aris, [Semantic substrates](https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2006.166), IEEE TVCG 2006 | full text |
| drag order | fractional index: "Reordering an object only involves editing a single value" | Wallace, [Realtime editing of ordered sequences](https://www.figma.com/blog/realtime-editing-of-ordered-sequences/), Figma 2017 | fetched |

**Taken from it on 2026-11-03**

- From Holt: R14's test builds the lift in set algebra and compares it with \`compile\`. It holds on the shop and on cb4. The one difference is kept on purpose (see Decisions).
- From Murphy et al.: \`compare\`, R12, chapter 6 and tutorial step 13. Their \`mapFunc\` takes "the first match of a given source model entity", the rule R11 already used for a foreign key naming several tables.
- From PivotGraph and from Graph Cube ([Zhao et al.](https://doi.org/10.1145/1989323.1989413), SIGMOD 2011, Def. 2): a line's weight is the sum over its base rows (R13). Before, a fold counted lines it merged, so two calls already merged into one line counted as one. Width now grows with the log of the weight.

**Where this tool differs**

- A fold set is not a Graph Cube cuboid. A cuboid replaces any subset of dimensions with \`*\`; a fold hides only the tail of the y list, chosen per subtree, and never an x level.
- PivotGraph rolls up two flat dimensions; levels here nest and may be ranks along a relation.
- Lakos's level (*Large-Scale C++ Software Design*, 1996) counts from the sinks, and cyclic components have no level. Both are from secondary sources, *not checked* in the book. Depth here counts from the sources and gives a cycle one rank, which is this tool's choice.

**Not taken, with the cost**

- Polaris concatenation (\`+\`): rows of different tables, each with its own levels, side by side on one axis. Today a row missing a level column is excluded (R5). Small.
- Edge attributes as levels: a relation's own columns for \`where\` and \`show\`. Nobre et al. ([survey](https://doi.org/10.1111/cgf.13728), CGF 2019) note faceting does not support them. Medium.
- Ordering an axis's values by barycentre or by hand (PivotGraph; Bertin's reorderable matrix, *not checked*). The \`order\` table reorders ties inside a cell only. Medium.
- Holt's "diagnostic" lowering as a gesture: the rows are on each line (R13) and shown on hover; a click that opens them is not built. Small.
- A stability test: adding rows keeps every pair's order on each axis. It holds for column levels by construction and fails for depth levels, where one new call can move a box deeper. The criterion is in the mental-map literature (Misue, Eades, Lai, Sugiyama, [Layout adjustment and the mental map](https://doi.org/10.1006/jvlc.1995.1010), 1995; its wording *not checked*). Small.
- SetCoLa ([Hoffswell, Borning, Heer](https://doi.org/10.1111/cgf.13440), EuroVis 2018): constraints on sets chosen by data properties. It needs a solver; Boundaries say ask first.

Citation only, not read: Koschke and Simon, [Hierarchical reflexion models](https://doi.org/10.1109/WCRE.2003.1287235), WCRE 2003, the next thing to read for a \`compare\` with nested claims; Wilkinson, *The Grammar of Graphics* (2005); Bertin, *Semiology of Graphics*; Eades and Feng's clustered graphs (GD '96).`
// %%
md`## Handover

### Where the work goes

- A new module, name not settled (open question). The engine is this spec's \`engine\` cell; \`tools/scratch/ordinal-diagram/engine.js\` is the same source with its bun tests.
- Tables to boxes and lines is this spec's \`relLib\` cell, the same source as \`tools/scratch/ordinal-diagram/rel.js\` (tests in \`rel.test.js\`). Added 2026-11-02; since 2026-11-03 every diagram on the page goes through it. \`oracle.js\` keeps the hand-written rows the page drew before, for the tests only.
- Read \`knowledge/svg-editor-architecture.md\` before adding editing to the SVG, and \`knowledge/keeping-user-state-in-the-saved-notebook.md\` before persisting the overlay table.

### Boundaries

| | |
|---|---|
| **always** | keep every iteration the SVG depends on sorted; run the checks below |
| **ask first** | adding a query language; adding any solver |
| **never** | write to snapshot tables from the diagram; use \`localeCompare\` or \`Math.random\` in layout |

### Checks

\`\`\`
bun test tools/scratch/ordinal-diagram/                 # 33 pass (engine.test.js, live.test.js, rel.test.js)
run_tests on @spec/ordinal-diagram                     # 12 test_* cells pass, offline
\`\`\`

### Steps

1. Settle the blocking questions below. **stop and show**
2. Move the engine into a module with the tests as its \`test_*\` cells.
3. Persist \`order\` and \`folds\` in the saved notebook.
4. Load a second, non-architecture snapshot and view to check the tool is schema-agnostic. **stop and show**`
// %%
md`## Open questions

A question marked blocking stops the handover.`
// %%
questions = [
  { q: "The axis template. The prototype reads boxes and lines from the tables' keys plus a JS list of levels. Tom described nested queries (..X, x, y) where each axis is a nesting of data transforms. Which form is the view definition?", blocking: true, answer: "" },
  { q: "Can the human key sit before data levels (overriding them), or only after them as a tie break? The prototype puts it last but one, so a drag cannot move a box out of its cell.", blocking: true, answer: "" },
  { q: "Rank within a relation level: longest path from sources (prototype), or distance from chosen roots (for a focus view)?", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Should a deterministic crossing-reduction pass order boxes inside a column before the id tie break? The prototype has none.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Data cycles: keep sharing one rank with an outline (prototype), or fold each cycle into a group automatically?", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "What is 'a dimension' as a constraint condition? Tom listed it beside edge label and entity type on 2026-10-26.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Should x-levels fold too (artboard_x_fold)? If both axes fold, two groups can claim the same cells.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Where does a promoted group live, given the snapshot is never edited: an overlay table in the notebook?", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "sql.js from a CDN (prototype, via stdlib SQLite) or vendored as a file attachment so the notebook works offline?", blocking: false, answer: "Neither. Tom, 2026-11-03: \"yes lets eliminate SQL now, it simplifies things\". SQL removed." },
  { q: "Rule rows (\"all stores below all services\", conditioned on relation, type or dimension) as a second overlay table, with cycles among them shown: in scope for the first module?", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Should edges be drawable only for relations the view names (drawRelations), with rank relations hidden by default?", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Module name.", blocking: false, answer: "" }
]
// %%
questionsTable = htl.html`<table style="font-size:.9em">
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>question</th><th>answer</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>${questions.map((q, i) => htl.html`<tr data-question=${i}><td>${q.blocking && !q.answer ? "blocking" : ""}</td><td>${q.q}</td><td>${q.answer || htl.html`<em>open</em>`}</td></tr>`)}</tbody>
</table>`
// %%
// mermaid draws dark text on no background, which is unreadable on a dark theme.
onPaper = (node) => htl.html`<div style="max-width:640px;background:#f6f7f9;border-radius:6px;padding:10px">${node}</div>`
// %%
specModule = "__SPEC_MODULE__"
// %%
import {md} from "@tomlarkworthy/editable-md"
// %%
import {svgLens} from "@tomlarkworthy/svg-lens"
// %%
import {annotation, annotations} from "@tomlarkworthy/annotate"
// %%
import {sticky} from "@tomlarkworthy/sticky"


